WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.842 --> 00:00:02.781
A client calls for a proposal.

2
00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:05.313
It wins on its first page.

3
00:00:06.328 --> 00:00:10.925
Start with their problem, in their numbers: 14 percent of jobs cancelled,

4
00:00:11.076 --> 00:00:12.353
target 8 percent.

5
00:00:13.414 --> 00:00:19.080
If the sponsor, who pays and owns it, thinks: yes, exactly, you are most of the way.

6
00:00:20.082 --> 00:00:21.498
Your method comes later.

7
00:00:22.881 --> 00:00:27.118
Then the outcome: the measure, the baseline, today's level before you start,

8
00:00:27.258 --> 00:00:29.069
the target, and who owns it.

9
00:00:30.286 --> 00:00:35.116
Then the approach, in phases, first a diagnostic phase: short and cheap,

10
00:00:35.278 --> 00:00:39.818
say three weeks, 12 days, 14,400 euros.

11
00:00:40.853 --> 00:00:45.393
It ends in a go/no-go decision: continue, change course, or stop.

12
00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:50.611
You price the rest after seeing the data: less risk for both sides.

13
00:00:51.946 --> 00:00:56.125
Write down the client commitments: the sponsor an hour every two weeks,

14
00:00:56.242 --> 00:00:57.972
a counterpart one day a week.

15
00:00:58.991 --> 00:01:03.089
Planning data in week one, and two sessions with the team leads.

16
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:07.643
Not small print: many slips start on the client side.

17
00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:13.914
Offer two or three options: diagnostic only, plus a pilot, or the full programme.

18
00:01:14.937 --> 00:01:19.384
With options the client chooses between yeses, not between yes and no.

19
00:01:20.366 --> 00:01:25.265
Five or six pages, and walk the sponsor through the outline by phone first.

20
00:01:26.626 --> 00:01:31.642
Proposals are often mostly about the consultancy, and hardly about the client.

21
00:01:32.698 --> 00:01:37.818
Purchasing compares day rates, so ask early how the choice is made, and by whom.

22
00:01:38.854 --> 00:01:44.264
On later phases, consultants differ: a range builds trust, a single number sticks.

23
00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.415
Ask before you write: if this succeeds, what changes in six months,

24
00:01:49.589 --> 00:01:50.843
and how will we see it?

25
00:01:52.213 --> 00:01:55.789
An example: an operations director calls Vantage.

26
00:01:56.547 --> 00:01:59.751
Changeovers on two thermoforming lines are slow.

27
00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:06.743
The OEE is around 58 percent of planned time: good parts at full speed.

28
00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:09.081
Budget: 60 thousand.

29
00:02:10.122 --> 00:02:11.005
The real worry:

30
00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:16.485
a large customer may leave if on-time delivery doesn't rise from 85 percent.

31
00:02:17.544 --> 00:02:20.876
And purchasing wants three quotes and a fixed price.

32
00:02:21.884 --> 00:02:25.599
Page one: that problem, the OEE as likely cause,

33
00:02:25.797 --> 00:02:29.326
and the outcome: above 95 percent by a named date.

34
00:02:30.382 --> 00:02:36.442
Phase one: a three-week diagnostic, 12 days, 14,400 euros fixed.

35
00:02:37.179 --> 00:02:39.977
Phase two: two options with a price range.

36
00:02:41.004 --> 00:02:45.903
Needed: data from the MES, the production engineer one day a week,

37
00:02:46.077 --> 00:02:47.981
the sponsor every two weeks.

38
00:02:49.005 --> 00:02:53.962
Within the 60 thousand, and call first: how will purchasing compare the quotes?

39
00:02:55.259 --> 00:02:56.606
Three ways to price.

40
00:02:57.394 --> 00:03:03.036
A day rate, say 1,200 euros: simple, the client carries the overrun,

41
00:03:03.222 --> 00:03:04.569
you are paid for hours.

42
00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.773
A fixed price, say 48 thousand, for an agreed scope: the client knows the bill,

43
00:03:11.028 --> 00:03:12.154
you carry the risk.

44
00:03:13.138 --> 00:03:15.449
Value-based pricing follows the worth:

45
00:03:15.565 --> 00:03:19.756
say 80 thousand for a change worth 900 thousand a year.

46
00:03:20.765 --> 00:03:25.003
It has the most upside, if the value is measurable, the baseline agreed,

47
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.127
and you control enough of the result.

48
00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:31.496
Advocates such as Alan Weiss find it the fairest.

49
00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:35.437
Purchasing often wants day rates: easy to compare.

50
00:03:36.452 --> 00:03:39.517
It works best with a client who already trusts you.

51
00:03:40.846 --> 00:03:47.290
A fixed price is a bet: you estimate 40 days, price 48 thousand, and it takes 50.

52
00:03:48.321 --> 00:03:52.187
Then your real rate drops to 960 euros a day.

53
00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:58.464
So add a risk margin, often 15 to 25 percent, for what you don't yet know.

54
00:03:59.487 --> 00:04:03.377
Write the scope tightly: deliverables, sessions, what is out.

55
00:04:04.446 --> 00:04:07.907
A written assumption that fails, like data in week one,

56
00:04:08.127 --> 00:04:10.147
is the basis for a change request.

57
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.671
Put it in a short written agreement, with days and price.

58
00:04:15.687 --> 00:04:16.836
One more analysis?

59
00:04:17.546 --> 00:04:21.238
Say kindly: happy to, but it's about three days outside.

60
00:04:21.972 --> 00:04:23.063
Add it, or swap?

61
00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:26.714
Free once sets everything after at zero.

62
00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:32.840
Discounts: ten percent off 1,200 is 1,080,

63
00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:35.893
about 11 percent more days for the same money.

64
00:04:36.884 --> 00:04:41.064
If you give one, trade it: a longer commitment, a named reference case,

65
00:04:41.215 --> 00:04:43.142
faster payment, or less scope.

66
00:04:44.232 --> 00:04:49.386
Never a bare discount, and don't cut phase one: it makes your price an opening bid.

67
00:04:50.411 --> 00:04:53.139
Asked for a discount: what would you like to leave out?

68
00:04:54.527 --> 00:05:00.158
A plant director wants less scrap on an extrusion line, 400 thousand euros a year,

69
00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:01.528
for a fixed price.

70
00:05:02.514 --> 00:05:08.713
You estimate 30 days if the quality data is clean, else 45; you know in week two.

71
00:05:09.732 --> 00:05:12.925
He mentions a competitor who quoted 30 thousand.

72
00:05:13.988 --> 00:05:19.131
Don't bet on clean: first 8 days fixed, nine thousand six hundred euros,

73
00:05:19.317 --> 00:05:20.594
data check included.

74
00:05:21.673 --> 00:05:28.233
Then phase two fixed on what you found: 22 or 37 days, plus a 15 percent margin.

75
00:05:29.226 --> 00:05:33.580
Or a value option: lower fixed fee plus a bonus if scrap falls,

76
00:05:33.708 --> 00:05:35.241
measured by his controller.

77
00:05:36.245 --> 00:05:37.615
And the 30 thousand?

78
00:05:38.366 --> 00:05:41.733
Compare what each quote delivers, and who carries the risk.
